<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
    <PropertyGroup>
        <BepInExPluginGuid>svenja001.gyk.graveyardkeeperaccessibility</BepInExPluginGuid>
        <BepInExPluginName>Graveyard Keeper Accessibility</BepInExPluginName>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <ItemGroup>
        <Compile Remove="..\Shared\**" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <!-- The keyboard reference travels with the mod. There is no Configuration Manager here (see
         ConfigManagerAccessibility), so this file is the only place a player can find out what the
         mod's keys are. Copied via the csproj rather than by adding a rule to the shared
         zip.target: OutputPath is already globbed into the release zip, so this ships without any
         tracked build file diverging from upstream. -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="KEYBINDINGS.md" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
        <!-- The install instructions ship too: a blind player who has just extracted the ZIP has
             the folder open in front of them, and that is the moment they need them. -->
        <None Include="..\..\README.md" Link="README.md" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
        <!-- GPL v3 requires the licence to accompany the distribution, and README.md links to it.
             Without this the link is dead for anyone who only ever sees the ZIP. -->
        <None Include="..\..\LICENSE" Link="LICENSE.txt" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <!-- Bundle the Prism native library so releases speak through NVDA/JAWS out of the box
         instead of falling back to SAPI. All three platforms ship because one release zip
         serves every storefront and we cannot know which OS it lands on; PrismWrapper picks
         the right file at runtime. MPL-2.0 requires the licence text to travel with the
         binaries; see libs\native\prism\README.md. -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <PrismFile Include="..\..\libs\native\prism\prism.dll" Link="prism.dll" />
        <PrismFile Include="..\..\libs\native\prism\libprism.so" Link="libprism.so" />
        <PrismFile Include="..\..\libs\native\prism\libprism.dylib" Link="libprism.dylib" />
        <PrismFile Include="..\..\libs\native\prism\LICENSE" Link="prism-LICENSE.txt" />
        <PrismFile Include="..\..\libs\native\prism\NOTICE" Link="prism-NOTICE.txt" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <!-- Tolk covers the one case Prism cannot: the GOG build of the game is a 32-bit process, and
         Prism has never published a 32-bit Windows library. Without this, GOG players get the
         Windows SAPI voice and no braille. Only the x86 build ships - on 64-bit Prism has already
         won, and TolkWrapper refuses to initialise there, so the two can never both hold the
         screen reader. nvdaControllerClient32.dll is Tolk's only on-disk dependency for the readers
         we cover; LGPL-3.0 requires the licence texts to travel with it, see
         libs\native\tolk\README.md. -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <TolkFile Include="..\..\libs\native\tolk\Tolk.dll" Link="Tolk.dll" />
        <TolkFile Include="..\..\libs\native\tolk\nvdaControllerClient32.dll" Link="nvdaControllerClient32.dll" />
        <TolkFile Include="..\..\libs\native\tolk\LICENSE.txt" Link="tolk-LICENSE.txt" />
        <TolkFile Include="..\..\libs\native\tolk\LICENSE-NVDA.txt" Link="nvdaControllerClient-LICENSE.txt" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <!-- Both sets land in OutputPath, which is where the plain (storefront-agnostic) ZIP and the
         local dev deploy are globbed from. The two bundled ZIPs then take one set each back out
         again - see ZipWithBepInEx. The Link metadata rides along into the None items, so each
         file's name inside the plugin folder is written down exactly once, and the release target
         reuses %(Link) rather than repeating the list. -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="@(PrismFile);@(TolkFile)" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <!--
        The same four documents again, this time at the ROOT of every release ZIP - the first
        thing in front of a player who has just extracted the download.

        The copies inside the plugin folder above stay where they are: someone who installs
        through Vortex, or who copies just the plugin folder into an existing setup, never sees
        the ZIP root at all. But that is also exactly why the plugin folder is the wrong place to
        rely on - it is four levels down, behind a name nobody has a reason to open, and a blind
        player extracting the archive has no folder listing to skim for it. Duplicating ~60 KB is
        the cheaper mistake.

        The names are prefixed rather than left bare because of where these roots are. The bundled
        ZIPs unpack into the GAME folder, which already receives BepInEx's own changelog.txt,
        BepInEx-LICENSE.txt and Doorstop-LICENSE.txt - a plain CHANGELOG.md or LICENSE.txt beside
        those would be genuinely ambiguous about what it documents. The prefix also keeps the four
        sorted together in a directory listing, which is how they are found when the listing is
        being read out one entry at a time.
    -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <RootDoc Include="..\..\README.md" Link="Accessibility-Mod-README.md" />
        <RootDoc Include="KEYBINDINGS.md" Link="Accessibility-Mod-KEYBINDINGS.md" />
        <RootDoc Include="CHANGELOG.md" Link="Accessibility-Mod-CHANGELOG.md" />
        <RootDoc Include="..\..\LICENSE" Link="Accessibility-Mod-LICENSE.txt" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
      <!-- Reference-only: without ExcludeAssets="runtime" the package's ~110 game and Unity
           assemblies (Assembly-CSharp.dll, UnityEngine.*.dll, ...) are copied to OutputPath.
           Harmless while OutputPath was a scratch folder, but OutputPath is now the game's
           BepInEx\plugins\<mod>\ - BepInEx would scan them, and shipped copies of
           Assembly-CSharp.dll shadow the real game assemblies. -->
      <PackageReference Include="GraveyardKeeper.GameLibs" Version="*-*" ExcludeAssets="runtime" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        Two more release ZIPs that carry BepInEx as well, so the whole install is one extract into
        the game folder - one with the 64-bit loader (Steam) and one with the 32-bit one (GOG).
        The standard zip.target ZIP is rooted at the game's BepInEx\ folder and assumes the loader
        is already there; these are rooted at the GAME folder, because Doorstop's winhttp.dll has
        to sit beside the .exe.

        Why bother: every extra manual step is a place a blind player ends up with a game that
        silently starts unmodded - wrong BepInEx asset, wrong folder, or forgetting the launch
        that creates BepInEx\plugins\. Shipping the loader removes all three at once; the ZIP
        already contains plugins\, so no first run is needed either.

        BepInEx and Doorstop are LGPL-2.1, which permits this as long as the binaries are
        unmodified and the licence texts travel with them - both conditions are met here, see
        libs\bepinex-dist\README.md. The mod's own GPL-3.0 is unaffected: two independent
        programs in one archive is mere aggregation.

        Runs after zip.target's ZipOutputPath so OutputPath is fully populated, and leaves that
        target completely alone - it is shared with ~47 other mods.
    -->
    <!--
        Rebuild the plain ZIP that zip.target just produced, and throw that one away.

        Two things are wrong with it as it stands. Its "_BepInEx" suffix means "rooted at the
        BepInEx folder", but sitting next to a "_WithBepInEx" ZIP that genuinely does contain
        BepInEx, it reads as the opposite of what it is - and this audience often picks a download
        by filename read aloud, with no folder listing to compare against. And its documentation is
        only in plugins\<mod>\, two levels below the root the player lands on.

        Restaged here rather than fixed in the shared zip.target, which ~47 other mods use, and
        rather than patched into the finished archive, which MSBuild has no task for. The glob and
        its exclusions are copied from that target deliberately: they must stay identical, so any
        drift is visible as a difference between two blocks in the same repository.
    -->
    <Target Name="RepackModOnlyZip" AfterTargets="ZipOutputPath" Condition="'$(Storefront)' != 'gog'">
        <PropertyGroup>
            <_ModStaging>$(TEMP)\GYK_staging_modonly\$(MSBuildProjectName)</_ModStaging>
            <_ModPluginStaging>$(_ModStaging)\plugins\$(MSBuildProjectName)</_ModPluginStaging>
            <_ModReleasesDir>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\AAA_Releases</_ModReleasesDir>
            <_PlainZip>$(_ModReleasesDir)\$(MSBuildProjectName)_v$(Version)_BepInEx.zip</_PlainZip>
        </PropertyGroup>

        <RemoveDir Directories="$(_ModStaging)" />
        <MakeDir Directories="$(_ModPluginStaging)" />

        <ItemGroup>
            <_ModOnlyFiles Include="$(OutputPath)\**\*.*"
                           Exclude="$(OutputPath)\**\*.zip;$(OutputPath)\**\*.pdb;$(OutputPath)\**\*.deps.json;$(OutputPath)\**\*.csv;$(OutputPath)\**\*.vortex_backup;$(OutputPath)\**\*.vortex_new;$(OutputPath)\SaveBackup\**\*.*;$(OutputPath)\_dump\**\*.*;$(OutputPath)\Locations\**\*.*" />
        </ItemGroup>

        <Copy SourceFiles="@(_ModOnlyFiles)"
              DestinationFiles="@(_ModOnlyFiles->'$(_ModPluginStaging)\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)')" />

        <!-- CHANGELOG.md never reaches OutputPath, so the glob above misses it. -->
        <Copy Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\CHANGELOG.md')"
              SourceFiles="$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\CHANGELOG.md"
              DestinationFolder="$(_ModPluginStaging)" />

        <!-- Root of this archive is the player's BepInEx\ folder. -->
        <Copy SourceFiles="@(RootDoc)" DestinationFiles="@(RootDoc->'$(_ModStaging)\%(Link)')" />

        <Error Condition="!Exists('$(_ModPluginStaging)\$(MSBuildProjectName).dll')"
               Text="Mod-only ZIP is missing the mod DLL." />
        <Error Condition="!Exists('$(_ModStaging)\Accessibility-Mod-README.md')"
               Text="Mod-only ZIP is missing its root documentation." />

        <ZipDirectory Overwrite="true"
                      SourceDirectory="$(_ModStaging)"
                      DestinationFile="$(_ModReleasesDir)\$(MSBuildProjectName)_v$(Version)_ModOnly.zip" />
        <Delete Files="$(_PlainZip)" />
        <RemoveDir Directories="$(_ModStaging)" />
        <Message Importance="high" Text="Mod-only release: $(MSBuildProjectName)_v$(Version)_ModOnly.zip" />
    </Target>

    <Target Name="ZipWithBepInEx" AfterTargets="ZipOutputPath" Condition="'$(Storefront)' != 'gog'">
        <PropertyGroup>
            <_FullStaging>$(TEMP)\GYK_staging_full\$(MSBuildProjectName)</_FullStaging>
            <_PluginStaging>$(_FullStaging)\BepInEx\plugins\$(MSBuildProjectName)</_PluginStaging>
            <_BepInExDist>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\libs\bepinex-dist</_BepInExDist>
            <_BepInExDistX86>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\libs\bepinex-dist-x86</_BepInExDistX86>
            <_ReleasesDir>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\AAA_Releases</_ReleasesDir>
        </PropertyGroup>

        <RemoveDir Directories="$(_FullStaging)" />
        <MakeDir Directories="$(_FullStaging)" />

        <ItemGroup>
            <!-- The loader, laid out relative to the game folder already. -->
            <_BepInExFiles Include="$(_BepInExDist)\**\*.*" Exclude="$(_BepInExDist)\README.md" />
            <!-- The mod itself, mirroring what went into the plain ZIP. Same exclusions as
                 zip.target: Vortex leftovers and build by-products must not ship. -->
            <_ModFiles Include="$(OutputPath)\**\*.*"
                       Exclude="$(OutputPath)\**\*.zip;$(OutputPath)\**\*.pdb;$(OutputPath)\**\*.deps.json;$(OutputPath)\**\*.csv;$(OutputPath)\**\*.vortex_backup;$(OutputPath)\**\*.vortex_new;$(OutputPath)\SaveBackup\**\*.*;$(OutputPath)\_dump\**\*.*;$(OutputPath)\Locations\**\*.*" />
            <!-- The two speech libraries as they sit in the staging dir, named from the same
                 %(Link) metadata that put them in OutputPath in the first place. Each bundle keeps
                 exactly one set; see the comment above the first ZipDirectory. -->
            <_StagedPrism Include="@(PrismFile->'$(_PluginStaging)\%(Link)')" />
            <_StagedTolk Include="@(TolkFile->'$(_PluginStaging)\%(Link)')" />
        </ItemGroup>

        <Copy SourceFiles="@(_BepInExFiles)"
              DestinationFiles="@(_BepInExFiles->'$(_FullStaging)\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)')" />
        <Copy SourceFiles="@(_ModFiles)"
              DestinationFiles="@(_ModFiles->'$(_PluginStaging)\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)')" />

        <!-- CHANGELOG.md never reaches OutputPath - zip.target copies it into its own staging dir
             directly - so the glob above misses it and this ZIP would ship without it. -->
        <Copy Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\CHANGELOG.md')"
              SourceFiles="$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\CHANGELOG.md"
              DestinationFolder="$(_PluginStaging)" />

        <!-- Root of these archives is the GAME folder, the one holding Graveyard Keeper.exe -
             which is where the player is standing the moment the extract finishes. Copied once
             here and left alone afterwards: both bundles are zipped out of this same staging dir,
             and the documentation is identical in each. -->
        <Copy SourceFiles="@(RootDoc)" DestinationFiles="@(RootDoc->'$(_FullStaging)\%(Link)')" />

        <!-- Fail loudly rather than shipping a ZIP that quietly cannot load anything. -->
        <Error Condition="!Exists('$(_FullStaging)\Accessibility-Mod-README.md')"
               Text="Bundled ZIP is missing its root documentation." />
        <Error Condition="!Exists('$(_FullStaging)\winhttp.dll')"
               Text="BepInEx bundle is missing winhttp.dll - is libs\bepinex-dist populated? See its README." />
        <Error Condition="!Exists('$(_PluginStaging)\$(MSBuildProjectName).dll')"
               Text="BepInEx bundle is missing the mod DLL." />

        <!--
            Each bundled ZIP carries only the speech library its architecture can actually load.
            Both are in the staging dir at this point (they were globbed out of OutputPath), so the
            64-bit bundle drops Tolk here and the 32-bit one below puts it back and drops Prism.

            A player never chooses between the two libraries and never sees them, so an unusable one
            in the folder is pure weight: Prism is x86-64 and its Linux and macOS builds are 64-bit
            too, all ~3.5 MB of it dead in a 32-bit install, and Tolk is x86, dead in a 64-bit one.
            It is not just size either - a folder that contains both invites the guess that the mod
            picks between them at runtime, when in fact bitness decides and TolkWrapper refuses to
            start on 64-bit at all.

            The storefront-agnostic ModOnly ZIP still ships both, deliberately: it is the download
            for players who already have BepInEx, and nothing in it says which store the game came
            from, so it has to work either way.
        -->
        <Delete Files="@(_StagedTolk)" />
        <Error Condition="!Exists('$(_PluginStaging)\prism.dll')"
               Text="64-bit bundle is missing prism.dll - is libs\native\prism populated? See its README." />

        <ZipDirectory Overwrite="true"
                      SourceDirectory="$(_FullStaging)"
                      DestinationFile="$(_ReleasesDir)\$(MSBuildProjectName)_v$(Version)_WithBepInEx.zip" />
        <Message Importance="high" Text="Bundled release: $(MSBuildProjectName)_v$(Version)_WithBepInEx.zip" />

        <!--
            Same bundle again for the GOG build, which is a 32-bit process and therefore cannot
            load the 64-bit Doorstop above: Windows falls through to the system winhttp.dll and the
            game starts unmodded with no error and no log at all. v0.1.0 shipped only the x64
            loader and reached every GOG player as a silent no-op.

            winhttp.dll is the ONLY file that differs between BepInEx's win_x86 and win_x64 assets
            (everything else, BepInEx\core\ included, is managed AnyCPU and byte-identical - hashed
            file by file, see libs\bepinex-dist-x86\README.md). So swap that one file in the staging
            dir already built above and zip it a second time, rather than staging twice: apart from
            the loader and the speech library - the two things that are architecture-specific by
            definition, and both swapped right here - the archives then cannot drift apart.
        -->
        <Error Condition="!Exists('$(_BepInExDistX86)\winhttp.dll')"
               Text="32-bit loader missing - is libs\bepinex-dist-x86 populated? See its README." />
        <Copy SourceFiles="$(_BepInExDistX86)\winhttp.dll" DestinationFolder="$(_FullStaging)" />

        <!-- Prism out, Tolk back in: the mirror image of the swap made before the ZIP above. -->
        <Delete Files="@(_StagedPrism)" />
        <Copy SourceFiles="@(TolkFile)" DestinationFiles="@(TolkFile->'$(_PluginStaging)\%(Link)')" />
        <Error Condition="!Exists('$(_PluginStaging)\Tolk.dll')"
               Text="32-bit bundle is missing Tolk.dll - is libs\native\tolk populated? See its README." />

        <ZipDirectory Overwrite="true"
                      SourceDirectory="$(_FullStaging)"
                      DestinationFile="$(_ReleasesDir)\$(MSBuildProjectName)_v$(Version)_WithBepInEx_GOG_32bit.zip" />
        <Message Importance="high" Text="Bundled release: $(MSBuildProjectName)_v$(Version)_WithBepInEx_GOG_32bit.zip" />

        <RemoveDir Directories="$(_FullStaging)" />
    </Target>
</Project>
